<body><main>
<nav><a href="./">← Chloe Reads Jon</a><span>EXHIBIT 029 / SMALL SCIENCE</span></nav>
<header class="hero"><div><div class="eyebrow">THE EVERYDAY WONDER DEPARTMENT</div><h1>The Tiny<br><em>Waterworks.</em></h1><p class="intro">Ice, a puddle, a puff of vapour. Same little molecule. Very different social lives.</p><p class="small">TURN THE HEAT. TUG A MOLECULE. SEE WHAT STAYS TOGETHER.</p></div><figure><img src="?art=1" alt="Original paper-diorama illustration of three jars: ordered water molecules in ice, loosely packed liquid, and widely separated intact molecules in vapour." width="1536" height="1024"><span class="stamp">H₂O<br>STILL H₂O</span><figcaption>A little field guide: ice → liquid → vapour.<br>Original GPT Image illustration; the live model is below.</figcaption></figure></header>
<section class="lab" aria-label="Interactive water molecule playground">
<div class="viewport"><div class="topline label"><span id="stateLabel">01 / LIQUID WATER</span><span>12 MOLECULES · 24 O–H BONDS</span></div>
<canvas id="tank" tabindex="0" role="img" aria-label="Water molecule model. Drag molecules with a pointer, or use the Nudge molecule button. Solid bonds inside each molecule stay intact in every phase."></canvas>
<div class="legend"><span><i class="solid"></i>Covalent: inside H₂O</span><span><i class="dashed"></i>Hydrogen: between H₂O</span></div></div>
<div class="controls"><h2>Your tiny climate.</h2>
<div><div class="range-title"><label for="temperature">Temperature</label><output for="temperature" id="tempOutput">25°C</output></div><input id="temperature" type="range" min="-20" max="120" value="25" step="1" aria-describedby="modelNote"><div class="endpoints small"><span>−20°C</span><span>120°C</span></div></div>
<div class="preset" aria-label="Phase presets"><button data-temp="-15">Ice</button><button data-temp="25" aria-pressed="true">Liquid</button><button data-temp="110">Vapour</button></div>
<p class="phase-copy" id="phaseCopy" aria-live="polite"></p>
<div class="toggles"><label><input id="bonds" type="checkbox" checked> Show hydrogen bonds</label><label><input id="labels" type="checkbox" checked> Label the atoms</label><div class="pair"><button id="pause">Pause motion</button><button id="nudge">Nudge molecule</button></div></div>
<p class="note" id="modelNote">A simplified 2D model at roughly 1 atmosphere, not a physics simulation. Phase changes are stylised; bond lengths, angles, speeds and spacing are illustrative.</p>
</div></section>
<div class="bottom"><section class="question" aria-labelledby="quizTitle"><div class="eyebrow">THE KETTLE TEST <span id="quizCount">/ 1 OF 4</span></div><h2 id="quizTitle">What does boiling actually break?</h2><p id="quizPrompt"></p><div class="answers" id="answers"></div><p class="feedback" id="feedback" aria-live="polite"></p><button id="next" class="primary" hidden>Next experiment →</button><div class="progress" id="progress" aria-label="0 of 4 experiments complete"></div><div id="certificate" class="success" hidden><b>Kettle detective, certified.</b><p>You kept the molecules intact and caught the sneaky bonding mix-up. Tea is safe in your hands.</p><button id="restart">Try the experiments again</button></div></section>
<section aria-labelledby="lookTitle"><div class="eyebrow">WHAT TO LOOK FOR</div><h2 id="lookTitle">Two kinds of together.</h2><div class="fact"><span class="number">01</span><div><b>Inside: a strong partnership.</b><p>Oxygen shares electrons with two hydrogens. These polar covalent O–H bonds hold a water molecule together. Normal melting and boiling leave them intact.</p></div></div><div class="fact"><span class="number">02</span><div><b>Between: a looser handshake.</b><p>A slightly positive hydrogen on one molecule is attracted to the slightly negative oxygen on a neighbour. These hydrogen bonds continually rearrange in liquid water.</p></div></div><div class="fact"><span class="number">03</span><div><b>Ice leaves elbow room.</b><p>Hydrogen bonding makes an open, three-dimensional network in real ice. This model spreads the molecules out to hint at why ice is less dense than liquid water and floats.</p></div></div></section></div>
<footer><p>Inspired by Jon's <a href="?source=1">2004 note on covalent and hydrogen bonding</a>, which pointed to a graphic explaining the difference.</p><p>One small question to take to the kitchen: if boiling split water into hydrogen and oxygen, where would the water go?</p></footer>
<noscript><p>This playground needs JavaScript. The explanation and illustrated field guide still work without it.</p></noscript>
</main><script>
'use strict';
const $=id=>document.getElementById(id),canvas=$('tank'),ctx=canvas.getContext('2d');
const reduced=matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;
let temperature=25,phase='liquid',paused=reduced,time=0,selected=-1,drag=-1,frameTime=0,points=[],W=700,H=410,nudgeOffset={x:0,y:0},bondPairs=[];
const descriptions={
ice:'An open arrangement. Neighbours hold their places while the molecules gently jiggle. Each H₂O stays whole.',
liquid:'Close neighbours, changing handshakes. Molecules move around and hydrogen bonds keep rearranging.',
vapour:'Room to roam. Molecules are far apart; the O–H bonds inside each one are still there.'
};
function unit(){const a=new Uint32Array(1);crypto.getRandomValues(a);return a[0]/4294967296}
const seeds=Array.from({length:12},()=>({p:unit()*6.28,v:.5+unit(),a:unit()*6.28}));
function targets(){
const scale=Math.min(W/700,H/410),space=(phase==='ice'?90:phase==='liquid'?73:145)*scale;
return seeds.map((s,i)=>{let col=i%4,row=Math.floor(i/4),x=W/2+(col-1.5)*space,y=H/2+(row-1)*space;
if(phase==='vapour'){x=42+(W-84)*((col+.5)/4);y=48+(H-96)*((row+.5)/3);}
return {x,y};});
}
function setTemp(value){
temperature=Number(value);const next=temperature<0?'ice':temperature>=100?'vapour':'liquid';phase=next;
$('temperature').value=temperature;$('tempOutput').textContent=temperature+'°C';
$('stateLabel').textContent=phase==='ice'?'01 / ICE':phase==='liquid'?'02 / LIQUID WATER':'03 / WATER VAPOUR';
$('phaseCopy').textContent=descriptions[phase];
document.querySelectorAll('[data-temp]').forEach(b=>b.setAttribute('aria-pressed',String(Number(b.dataset.temp)===temperature)));
}
function resize(){const r=canvas.getBoundingClientRect();W=r.width;H=r.height;const d=Math.min(devicePixelRatio||1,2);canvas.width=W*d;canvas.height=H*d;ctx.setTransform(d,0,0,d,0,0);points=targets().map((p,i)=>({...p,a:seeds[i].a}));}
function atom(x,y,r,fill,label){ctx.beginPath();ctx.arc(x,y,r,0,Math.PI*2);ctx.fillStyle=fill;ctx.fill();ctx.strokeStyle='#0b292c';ctx.lineWidth=1.3;ctx.stroke();if($('labels').checked){ctx.fillStyle=label==='O'?'#fff9ec':'#163c3e';ctx.font='bold '+(r<10?10:12)+'px Georgia';ctx.textAlign='center';ctx.textBaseline='middle';ctx.fillText(label,x,y+.5)}}
function render(now){
const dt=Math.min((now-frameTime)/1000||0,.04);frameTime=now;
if(!paused&&!document.hidden)time+=dt;
const ts=targets(),size=W<400? .80:1;
points.forEach((p,i)=>{
if(i===drag)return;const s=seeds[i],t=ts[i],amp=phase==='ice'?2:phase==='liquid'?13:22,speed=phase==='ice'?.8:phase==='liquid'?1.2:2.2;
const ox=i===selected?nudgeOffset.x:0,oy=i===selected?nudgeOffset.y:0;
const tx=Math.max(30,Math.min(W-30,t.x+Math.sin(time*speed*s.v+s.p)*amp+ox));
const ty=Math.max(30,Math.min(H-30,t.y+Math.cos(time*speed*.7+s.p)*amp+oy));
p.x+=(tx-p.x)*Math.min(1,dt*8);p.y+=(ty-p.y)*Math.min(1,dt*8);p.a=s.a+(phase==='ice'?.03:.35)*Math.sin(time*.6+s.p);
});
ctx.clearRect(0,0,W,H);ctx.fillStyle='#113d41';ctx.fillRect(0,0,W,H);
ctx.fillStyle='#ffffff11';for(let x=16;x<W;x+=24)for(let y=16;y<H;y+=24)ctx.fillRect(x,y,1,1);
const hydrogens=p=>[p.a-.912,p.a+.912].map(a=>({x:p.x+Math.cos(a)*25*size,y:p.y+Math.sin(a)*25*size}));
bondPairs=[];
if(phase!=='vapour'){
const candidates=[];
points.forEach((p,i)=>hydrogens(p).forEach((h,k)=>{let best=null;points.forEach((q,j)=>{if(i===j)return;const d=Math.hypot(h.x-q.x,h.y-q.y);if(d>26*size&&d<90*size&&(!best||d<best.d))best={i,j,k,h,q,d};});if(best)candidates.push(best)}));
candidates.forEach(b=>{if(bondPairs.filter(a=>a.j===b.j).length<2)bondPairs.push(b)});
}
if($('bonds').checked){ctx.setLineDash([4,5]);ctx.lineWidth=1.6;ctx.strokeStyle='#86d2cd';bondPairs.forEach(b=>{ctx.beginPath();ctx.moveTo(b.h.x,b.h.y);ctx.lineTo(b.q.x,b.q.y);ctx.stroke()});ctx.setLineDash([]);}
points.forEach((p,i)=>{
if(i===selected){ctx.beginPath();ctx.arc(p.x,p.y,38*size,0,6.29);ctx.lineWidth=1;ctx.strokeStyle='#f2c35e';ctx.stroke();}
const hs=hydrogens(p);ctx.strokeStyle='#f2c35e';ctx.lineWidth=5*size;ctx.lineCap='round';hs.forEach(h=>{ctx.beginPath();ctx.moveTo(p.x,p.y);ctx.lineTo(h.x,h.y);ctx.stroke()});atom(p.x,p.y,16*size,'#db533b','O');hs.forEach(h=>atom(h.x,h.y,10*size,'#f5edda','H'));
});
ctx.fillStyle='#bad9d5';ctx.font='12px "Courier New"';ctx.textAlign='left';ctx.textBaseline='alphabetic';ctx.fillText(drag>=0?'Your molecule is still H₂O.':paused?'Motion paused · controls still work':'Drag any molecule. Its atoms come along.',14,H-14);
requestAnimationFrame(render);
}
$('temperature').addEventListener('input',e=>setTemp(e.target.value));
document.querySelectorAll('[data-temp]').forEach(b=>b.addEventListener('click',()=>{nudgeOffset={x:0,y:0};setTemp(b.dataset.temp)}));
function updatePause(){$('pause').textContent=paused?'Resume motion':'Pause motion';$('pause').setAttribute('aria-pressed',String(paused))}
$('pause').onclick=()=>{paused=!paused;updatePause()};
$('nudge').onclick=()=>{selected=(selected+1)%12;nudgeOffset={x:(selected%2?1:-1)*36,y:-32};};
function pointer(e){const r=canvas.getBoundingClientRect();return{x:e.clientX-r.left,y:e.clientY-r.top}}
canvas.addEventListener('pointerdown',e=>{const p=pointer(e);let best=42;points.forEach((q,i)=>{const d=Math.hypot(p.x-q.x,p.y-q.y);if(d<best){best=d;drag=i;selected=i;}});if(drag>=0)canvas.setPointerCapture(e.pointerId)});
canvas.addEventListener('pointermove',e=>{if(drag<0)return;const p=pointer(e);points[drag].x=Math.max(30,Math.min(W-30,p.x));points[drag].y=Math.max(30,Math.min(H-30,p.y));});
function release(){drag=-1;nudgeOffset={x:0,y:0}}
canvas.addEventListener('pointerup',release);canvas.addEventListener('pointercancel',release);
canvas.addEventListener('keydown',e=>{if(e.key==='Enter'||e.key===' '){e.preventDefault();$('nudge').click()}});
const experiments=[
{title:'What does boiling actually break?',prompt:'Set the model to vapour. What lets the molecules drift apart?',temp:110,answers:['The O–H bonds inside each molecule.','Hydrogen bonds between neighbouring molecules.','The oxygen atoms themselves.'],correct:1,why:'Yes. Boiling overcomes the attractions between molecules. The strong covalent O–H bonds stay intact, so water vapour is still H₂O.'},
{title:'Where is the stronger bond?',prompt:'Follow a gold solid line from O to H. What are you looking at?',temp:25,answers:['A covalent bond within one water molecule.','A hydrogen bond between two molecules.','A line with no chemical meaning.'],correct:0,why:'Exactly. A covalent O–H bond involves shared electrons inside one molecule. The dashed teal lines show the weaker attractions between molecules.'},
{title:'Why does the ice float?',prompt:'Switch between ice and liquid. In real ice, the hydrogen-bond network has an open structure. What follows?',temp:-15,answers:['Ice has fewer atoms in each H₂O molecule.','Freezing turns oxygen into hydrogen.','The same amount of water takes up more space.'],correct:2,why:'Right. The open ice structure occupies more volume for the same mass, so ice is less dense and floats. Our flat model only hints at that three-dimensional structure.'},
{title:'Can a kettle make rocket fuel?',prompt:'You boil pure water and collect its vapour. What have you collected?',temp:110,answers:['A mixture of hydrogen and oxygen gas.','Whole water molecules in the gas phase.','Only oxygen, since hydrogen is lighter.'],correct:1,why:'Still water! Splitting water into hydrogen and oxygen is a chemical change, for example through electrolysis. A kitchen kettle causes a physical change of phase.'}
];
let q=0,solved=false;
function showQuestion(){const x=experiments[q];solved=false;$('quizCount').textContent='/ '+(q+1)+' OF 4';$('quizTitle').textContent=x.title;$('quizPrompt').textContent=x.prompt;$('feedback').textContent='';$('next').hidden=true;$('answers').replaceChildren();x.answers.forEach((s,i)=>{const b=document.createElement('button');b.textContent=s;b.onclick=()=>choose(i,b);$('answers').append(b)});setTemp(x.temp);paintProgress();}
function paintProgress(){const n=q+(solved?1:0);$('progress').replaceChildren();for(let i=0;i<4;i++){const d=document.createElement('span');d.className='dot'+(i<n?' done':'');$('progress').append(d)}$('progress').setAttribute('aria-label',n+' of 4 experiments complete')}
function choose(i,b){if(solved)return;const x=experiments[q];if(i!==x.correct){$('feedback').textContent='Not quite. '+(q===0?'Watch the gold O–H lines: those stay attached.':q===1?'Look inside a single O–H–H group, rather than between groups.':q===2?'Every molecule keeps its two hydrogens. Look at the space between neighbours.':'The gold bonds have not broken. Which answer keeps each H₂O together?');return;}solved=true;b.classList.add('chosen');$('answers').querySelectorAll('button').forEach(el=>el.disabled=true);$('feedback').textContent=x.why;paintProgress();$('next').hidden=false;$('next').textContent=q===3?'Collect your certificate →':'Next experiment →';}
$('next').onclick=()=>{if(!solved)return;if(q<3){q++;showQuestion();$('quizTitle').scrollIntoView({block:'nearest',behavior:reduced?'instant':'smooth'});$('answers').querySelector('button').focus({preventScroll:true})}else{$('next').hidden=true;$('certificate').hidden=false;$('restart').focus({preventScroll:true})}};
$('restart').onclick=()=>{q=0;$('certificate').hidden=true;showQuestion()};
setTemp(25);updatePause();resize();showQuestion();setTemp(25);new ResizeObserver(resize).observe(canvas);requestAnimationFrame(render);
</script></body></html>
